\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary Forint Dn Vs Dlr; Finance Minister Comments Ignored \b0\par\par\uc2 256 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1997\par 12:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST (AP-Dow Jones)--The Hungarian forint is weakening against the dollar Monday and is slightly weaker against its fixing basket, with remarks from Minister of Finance Peter Medgyessy having little impact on the forint. \par \par \uc2  At 1002 GMT, traders quoted the dollar at 196.48 to 196.53 forints, up from 195.61 to 195.66 forints in mid-session trading Friday. They quoted the mark at 109.72 to 109.77 forints, down slightly from 109.80 to 109.85 forints. \par \par \uc2  The forint has weakened a bit since the opening from its strongest point on the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band of 4.5%, which allows the forint to move 2.25% either way. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses a fixing basket made up of 30% dollar and 70% mark to establish the band. \par \par \uc2  At around 0955 GMT the forint was seen at about 2.21% from the midpoint, but Peter Luczo, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Inter-Europa Bank in Budapest, said the weakening wasn't significant and was just a result of changes in the dollar-mark rate, which the forint tracks closely. \par \par \uc2  Luczo said remarks by Hungarian Minister of Finance Peter Medgyessy earlier Monday about a possible widening of the forint's trading band, as well as the possibility of a cut in the 1.0% rate of the forint's devaluation depending on September inflation figures, didn't surprise the market. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 22-09-97 \par \par \uc2  1013GMT \par \par \uc2  1168 1868 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt9m078po\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\sh