\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary Forint Still Weak Vs Basket, Czech Effect Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 1997\par 12:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST (AP-Dow Jones)--Waves from the Czech Republic's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market arrived as ripples in Hungary Tuesday, with uncertainties over the Czech koruna seen contributing to a continued weakness of the Hungarian forint against its fixing basket. \par \par \uc2  The forint was trading at shortly before 1000 GMT at about 20 fillers from its strongest point on the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, roughly the same point at which it traded during the middle of Monday's session. \par \par \uc2  'The forints was 20 fillers over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bid, which is unusual and can be attributed to an effect from the Czech market,' said trader Szabolcs Brezina at Commercial Bank in Budapest. \par \par \uc2  There are 100 fillers in a forint. \par \par \uc2  The forint's weakening is also a result of the dollar's gains against the Deutsche mark on international markets, Brezina said. \par \par \uc2  At 1007 GMT, traders quoted the dollar at 181.70 to 181.75 forints, up from 180.90 to 180.95 forints in mid-session trading Monday. They quoted the mark at 107.03 to 107.06 forints, down from 107.30 to 107.35 forints. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 27-05-97 \par \par \uc2  1010GMT \par \par \uc2  1168 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5r03ut5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
